## When Resuming for a Fixed Implementation

<%= it.variant({
  "claude-code": "The trigger is `RE_EVALUATE` from `team-lead`. DM new findings per Step 5 during each resume round.\n",
  codex: "When the orchestrator re-engages you — spawning you again with the re-evaluation context, or a follow-up message — this is a continuation of your analysis. Record new findings per Step 5 and report a fresh verdict at the end.\n",
  opencode: "When the orchestrator re-engages you — spawning you again with the re-evaluation context, or a follow-up message — this is a continuation of your analysis. Record new findings per Step 5 and report a fresh verdict at the end.\n",
  antigravity: "The trigger is `RE_EVALUATE` from `team-lead`. DM new findings per Step 5 during each resume round.\n"
}) %>
### 1. Review What Changed

<%= it.variant({
  "claude-code": "The orchestrator's re-evaluation DM gives you the fix commit range, a plain account of what changed and why, and anything the wave could not fix — use them, with the commits, to decide which user entry points to re-exercise. The implementation running at HEAD is ground truth.\n",
  codex: "The orchestrator's re-evaluation context gives you the fix commit range, a plain account of what changed and why, and anything the wave could not fix — use them, with the commits, to decide which user entry points to re-exercise. The implementation running at HEAD is ground truth.\n",
  opencode: "The orchestrator's re-evaluation context gives you the fix commit range, a plain account of what changed and why, and anything the wave could not fix — use them, with the commits, to decide which user entry points to re-exercise. The implementation running at HEAD is ground truth.\n",
  antigravity: "The orchestrator's re-evaluation DM gives you the fix commit range, a plain account of what changed and why, and anything the wave could not fix — use them, with the commits, to decide which user entry points to re-exercise. The implementation running at HEAD is ground truth.\n"
}) %>
Tag findings you raise during this round with the new round number (e.g., `FINDING: <label> round-2`).

### 2. Triage Each Prior Failure

<%= it.variant({
  "claude-code": "For every failure you raised last round, determine its current status from the commits and the orchestrator's account of what the wave could not fix — re-enter at the user entry point to confirm, never assume from the account alone:\n",
  codex: "For every failure you raised in the previous round, determine its current status from the commits and the orchestrator's account of what the wave could not fix — re-enter at the relevant user entry point to confirm, never assume from the account alone:\n",
  opencode: "For every failure you raised in the previous round, determine its current status from the commits and the orchestrator's account of what the wave could not fix — re-enter at the relevant user entry point to confirm, never assume from the account alone:\n",
  antigravity: "For every failure you raised last round, determine its current status from the commits and the orchestrator's account of what the wave could not fix — re-enter at the user entry point to confirm, never assume from the account alone:\n"
}) %>
- **Addressed**: Re-running every witness-matrix entry at the new HEAD produces the working outcome and each is pinned by a green in-suite control. A code fix that resolves the internal issue may still produce a wrong user outcome — do not accept the fix at face value.
- **Partially addressed**: The user-facing failure is reduced but not gone, or the fix shifted it to a different surface. A fix that repairs the flagged instance of a class finding while siblings remain is partially addressed. State what the user still observes and why it still matters.
- **Unaddressed**: No commit resolves it, or the orchestrator flagged it as not fixed — re-state it with the same weight.

### 3. Extend Questions and Check for New Failures

<%= it.variant({
  "claude-code": "Fix code may introduce new user-facing failures adjacent to the original — re-exercise any user paths the fix touches, not only the paths directly targeted. Extend the question set with anything the fix reveals.\n",
  codex: "Fix code may introduce new user-facing failures adjacent to the original. Re-exercise any user paths the fix touches, not only the paths directly targeted. Extend the question set with anything the fix reveals; approval still requires every gating question answered.\n",
  opencode: "Fix code may introduce new user-facing failures adjacent to the original. Re-exercise any user paths the fix touches, not only the paths directly targeted. Extend the question set with anything the fix reveals; approval still requires every gating question answered.\n",
  antigravity: "Fix code may introduce new user-facing failures adjacent to the original — re-exercise any user paths the fix touches, not only the paths directly targeted. Extend the question set with anything the fix reveals.\n"
}) %>
<%= it.variant({
